The problem is from a GATE exam. We have two relations: R(P, Q) and S(X, Y). The expression E is: E = { <u> | ∃v ∃w <u, v> ∈ R ∧ <v, w> ∈ S }
I’ve got this relational database question from a GATE exam. There are two relations: R with attributes P and Q, and S with X and Y. The tuple relational calculus expression E is the set of tuples <u> where there’s some v and w such that <u, v> is in R and < <v, w> is in S. So, basically, u is the P attribute from R, and v (the Q attribute) must match the X attribute in S for some tuple.

This means we’re looking for all P values in R where the corresponding Q value equals some X in S.

